# DILIH — DropItLikeItsHot

**DILIH** runs the Xbox Series build of a console title natively on Windows, from
files you supply yourself. It started as a fork of VIctory and is now its own
project. Its main work in progress is a split-memory mode that treats the
console's single shared memory as two (system RAM and the graphics card's VRAM),
so data moves only when the other side actually needs it; alongside it, our own
speed and graphics work.

- The executable is **`dilih.exe`** (`cargo build --release -p victory`).
- Switches can be set as **`DILIH_*`**: `DILIH_X=1` sets `VICTORY_X=1` (and wins
  over it). The code and its documentation keep upstream's `VICTORY_*` names and
  the `victory-*` crates, so ports from upstream VIctory and its forks merge cleanly.
- Saves, the shader cache and the pipeline library live in `%LOCALAPPDATA%\dilih`
  (an existing `%LOCALAPPDATA%ictory` is moved there on the first start).

**This repository contains no game files, code or assets.** You need your own
copy of the game. Not affiliated with or endorsed by the game's developer or
publisher.

> **Based on** [victory-fork](https://github.com/fxceless/victory-fork) (formerly
> victory-gta6) by fxceless, itself a fork of [VIctory](https://github.com/Marvisak/VIctory) by Marvisak and
> contributors, licensed GPL-3.0-or-later (see `LICENSE`); their full history is kept below. It also merges
> MyVictory-Fork (MaDeInCCCP2), asdaasdsaddf/VIctory `test/fx-prs`, FulGerNet/macca-cl, Trexies/victory-fork
> VIwork, ZSlayerHQ/victory-world-runtime, WillyboPeep/victory-all-forks and others (see the merge commits).
> This repository's earlier line, based on [XCL](https://github.com/ivall/xcl) (GPL-2.0-only), is kept
> unchanged on the `xcl` branch and is not mixed into this one.

## Credits

- **[VIctory](https://github.com/Marvisak/VIctory)** by Marvisak and its
  contributors is the base of everything here: the loader, the runtime
  services, the Direct3D 12 back end and the shader recompiler. Licensed
  GPL-3.0-or-later; so is this fork. Upstream's history is kept intact below
  the single commit that carries this fork's changes.
- **[XCL](https://github.com/ivall/xcl)** by ivall and its contributors
  (GPL-2.0-only) was used as a reference for how the same title behaves under
  another layer. Fixes inspired by it were written anew in VIctory's code.
- DILIH's changes were developed with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code).

## Status (2026-11-25)

**Not playable yet: 2.5 to 5 frames per second, and shadowed areas are lit only by a flat stand-in.**
Tested on one machine: Windows 11, Intel i7-14700KF, NVIDIA RTX 4070 12 GB,
32 GB RAM. The Series X profile is used throughout.

| Stage | State in this fork |
|-------|--------------------|
| Boot, menus, start screen | works, 30 fps (as upstream) |
| Opening cutscene | plays from start to finish, in order, lit: title cards, credits cards, night and dusk shots |
| Saves | the game autosaves a checkpoint and loads it again |
| First gameplay scene (the prologue) | renders: characters, textures, sky, sunlit exterior; HUD, pause menu and pad input work; walking, the camera, the objective hints and the hold-LT interaction menu work; TALK with a cellmate (dialogue choices) and the next objective, following the guard out of the dormitory into the corridor, the hall and the gate to the yard, work; hair and faces mostly without the 8x8 blocks (some stepped dark edges remain around heads against bright windows) (RTX 5080, 2026-11-24) |
| Light in shadow | stand-in: the game's ray-traced bounce light and ray-traced lamp and sun shadows do not run yet; a flat ambient floor and lit shadow masks stand in for them (on by default, `VICTORY_AMBIENT_STUB=0` / `VICTORY_SHADOWMASK_STUB=0` turn them off, #30), so interiors are readable but flat |
| Frame rate in the world | 2-4 fps: 420-450 ms per frame without the relay (`VICTORY_RELAY=0`), 240-350 ms with it (the default now) or with `VICTORY_LATE_WAIT=3 VICTORY_WIDE_BACK=1 VICTORY_CARD_BUFFERS_MIN_MB=1 VICTORY_CARD_BUFFERS_MB=1024`; it started at 3-6 seconds per frame. RTX 5080, 2026-11-24: 226-290 ms by default once the shader cache is warm (the first run of a build that changes shaders waits about 7 minutes on some 2,100 pipeline builds); since texture staging buffers are only marked in flight when uploaded (a1f70e3): 208-262 ms in the prison (median 221; settles 13.8 -> 5.2 per frame), about 4-5 fps. With the VIwork port (#32), 2026-11-25: 224-226 ms median on the cutscene bench (204-213 before it); most of the difference is the scene it now draws, the cell walls and the window mesh: about 21 ms more on the card, 13 ms more preparing and 41 MB more copies a frame. The view guard is the part that draws them; `VICTORY_VIEW_GUARD=0` runs at 189 ms without them. Split memory stage 1 on by default (#48), 2026-11-25: 193 ms median (212-215 with `VICTORY_SPLIT_MEM_WC=0`): shader writes to write-combining title memory stay on the card, so copy-back falls from about 36 to 12 ms and copies from 165 to 82 MB a frame |
| Stability | no driver hang or device loss in normal runs (a hang introduced by the wave64 update was found and fixed: its barrier form for 64-lane programs is now off unless `VICTORY_WAVE_WIDE=1`) |
| Linux (Proton) | the workspace builds, tests and runs its file-side commands natively; the game via `proton/run.sh` is new and untested beyond smoke tests (docs/proton.md) |

### What this fork changed (summary)

Rendering:
- Meshlet culling read its scene buffers as the wrong kind of view, so the world drew almost nothing; fixed.
- Per-draw descriptor tables could run out, after which draws used slots never made for them and the card hung; fixed.
- Light packed by the game's compute shaders was read back through empty copies (sky, probes, fog); fixed for every mip level and layer.
- The fog reconstruction, shadow-mask, hair, blend-shape skinning, fire and several material programs did not translate; fixed.
- Gathers at a named mip level (with and without an offset) read level 0; fixed.
- Implicit-level samples outside pixel shaders produced invalid DXIL in 730 compute programs; fixed.
- Rectangle lists, depth and stencil twins for depth textures written by compute, texture read-backs.
- Ray tracing, in progress and off by default: the game's own structure-builder programs translate, title memory can be read by raw address from shaders, the hardware ray/box and ray/triangle test is emulated in DXIL (checked against a reference on the GPU), and 59 of the 64 trace programs translate. Nothing is lit by it yet. In the game it is off: every ray misses without reading memory (the emulated test is only the tests' reference; `VICTORY_RT_EMULATE=1` brings it back for comparison, #27). See [docs/fork-ray-tracing.md](docs/fork-ray-tracing.md).

Speed and memory:
- Waits for memory values, write-watch queries and page queries no longer walk page tables per call.
- Large title buffers get a copy in card memory, with shader writes found by hashing on the card.
- Submissions can be sent ahead of the wait for the card (`VICTORY_LATE_WAIT`), with a relay thread that lands results as the card finishes (`VICTORY_RELAY`, at stage 2 by default; `0` turns it off): in a first five-minute run the card's idle time behind the submitting thread fell from about 136 to 30 ms per frame.
- Title memory can be opened as a graphics heap so that buffers are used in place instead of copied (`VICTORY_INPLACE=1` windows outside buffers, `=2` title buffers too, `=3` also the big ones kept in the card's memory, off by default; buffers in place declare their shader writes so the relay waits for them and other copies see them; private memory, and the page pool with `VICTORY_POOL_COMMIT=1`, which makes its sections committed a chunk at a time (`VICTORY_POOL_CHUNK_MB`, 1024) so that a window there is a buffer in the section's heap; a probe confirmed the approach on the test GPU).
- The bindless texture queue no longer uploads stale views; the view table no longer grows without bound; the pipeline library is mapped instead of read into memory.
- An intermittent device loss was traced to the pipeline library file being replaced while mapped; fixed.

- Translated shaders get clean-up passes over the finished module (dead code, constant folding, reuse of values, branch facts); the pack's host instruction count falls by about 43 %, worth about 20 ms of GPU time per frame on the test machine (`VICTORY_SHADER_OPT=0` turns them off).

Most of the newer speed changes sit behind environment switches that are off
by default (`VICTORY_LATE_WAIT`, `VICTORY_WIDE_BACK`,
`VICTORY_CARD_BUFFERS_MIN_MB`, `VICTORY_INPLACE`, `VICTORY_BVH_BUILDER`, ...).
Each is described in a comment where it is read.

### Known problems

- Everything in shadow is lit by one flat grey (see above).
- The interior of the first gameplay room is sometimes not drawn after the cutscene hands over to gameplay.
- Around 24 GB of memory is in use during play.
- A few style lints are switched off for this fork in the workspace `Cargo.toml` until the newer code is tidied.
- Many changes here are specific to this title and this GPU, and some are marked `shortcut:` in the code where they approximate what the console does.

### Running on Linux

The workspace compiles, tests and runs its file-side commands (`config`,
`inspect`, `census`, `shader-info`) natively on Linux. The game itself needs
a Windows environment, which Proton supplies: build `victory.exe` with
`cargo win` and run it through `proton/run.sh`. Packages, commands, prefix
layout and known Wine gaps are in [docs/proton.md](docs/proton.md).

Everything below this line is upstream VIctory's own README, unchanged; its
status section describes upstream, not this fork.

---

# VIctory

Runs the title (Xbox Series X|S) natively on Windows, from files
the user supplies.

The title's x86-64 code runs unchanged inside our process; VIctory supplies
the platform underneath it and converts the title's data, ahead of time where
it can and on first use where it must (shaders). See
[docs/architecture.md](docs/architecture.md).

## Status (2026-11-23)

**Not playable yet.** The title boots, runs its menus at its own 30 fps and
loads the world, where rendering fails.

| Stage | State |
|-------|-------|
| Boot, first-boot screens (accessibility, language, settings, legal) | works, 30 fps |
| Intro video with sound | works |
| Start screen, profile and saves | works |
| Loading into the world (about 50 s) | works |
| The world (first mission's intro) | reached and runs with indirect draws; its title card renders, the scene after it is still dark; 0.5-1.8 s per frame |

Tested on one machine: Windows 11, AMD RX 6700 XT.

> **Warning.** Entering the world on a real GPU can currently hang it, and
> Windows then resets the display driver (every monitor goes black for a
> moment). To look further, use a diagnostics build with
> `VICTORY_D3D12_WARP=1`, which renders on the software adapter.

What each part does today and what it still needs:

| Subsystem | Today | Needs |
|-----------|-------|-------|
| Loader | Runs the title's executable and its DLLs in our process; crash reports name the faulting code and are kept in `crashes.log` | – |
| Runtime services | One signed-in offline user, licences, packages, saves that survive a kill at any step, text entry dialog | Speech is silent; the dialog and relaunch are untested live |
| Network | Offline by design: the title cannot reach the network | – |
| Files and streaming | Archives and streamed data read, zlib and BCPack decoded in parallel | Loading is limited by writes into title memory |
| Input | XInput pads, keyboard, keyboard stand-in pad | Trigger rumble |
| Audio | Menu, video and loading sound; XMA voices and Opus streams decode | Reverb is silent; Opus results unverified against the title |
| GPU: menus | Draws, compute and presentation on host D3D12; only changed title memory is copied | Frame pacing drifts on one screen in release builds |
| GPU: world | Mesh pipelines, shader-written buffers, descriptor lifetimes, indirect execution (arguments clamped), pipelines made by worker threads and kept on disk | Most of the scene is still missing (untranslated shaders drop draws); frames take seconds; real queries, depth bounds, HDR and scaling are missing |
| Shaders | Console GPU code recompiled to DXIL at first use, cached on disk. Menus: all. World: mesh 8,191 of 8,506, vertex/pixel 10,617 of 12,346, compute 1,643 of 2,539 | 64-bit image atomics, merged geometry stages, the ray-tracing builder's shaders; some compute programs need a GPU with 64-lane waves |
| Textures | Console tiled layouts and detiling for the modes the title uses | Render-target metadata planes |
| Series S mode | Runs through the menus to the loading screen | The world, as above |

## Getting started

Requirements: Windows 10/11 x64, a CPU with AVX2, a Direct3D 12 GPU with
shader model 6.6 (and mesh shaders for the world), Rust (pinned in
`rust-toolchain.toml`).

The title also needs several GiB of available Windows commit at startup.
Check **Committed** under **Task Manager → Performance → Memory**. If
`crashes.log` reports Windows error 1455, close other applications or increase
the Windows paging file size, then retry.

```powershell
cargo xtask install-hooks                # asset-policy check on every commit
copy victory.example.toml victory.toml   # then set your paths (outside the repo)
cargo test --workspace                   # needs no game data
cargo run -p victory -- config           # check your paths
```

On Windows systems with multiple GPUs, select the hardware adapter used by the
shader test harness with a case-insensitive substring of its DXGI name:

```powershell
$env:VICTORY_SHADER_ADAPTER = 'NVIDIA'
cargo test -p victory-shader --lib
Remove-Item Env:VICTORY_SHADER_ADAPTER
```

The default is the Windows-selected adapter. Explicit WARP runs still use WARP.

Shaders can be translated ahead of the first run (`translate --cache`).
Use the same cache directory and translation settings for warm-up and
play. Shader entries survive rebuilds outside the translator and its build
inputs. Changes to the translator or GPU shader capabilities select new
entries. Unsupported shader results are cached under the same rules.
`--pipelines` also stores supported pipelines from the shader pack
in the host D3D12 library, which is specific to the host GPU and driver.
Add `--used-pipelines` to prebuild only exact pipeline keys requested by
earlier runs in that cache. The shader warm-up still covers all input files.

```powershell
target\release\victory.exe translate --cache --pipelines <game dir>\shaders.rpf <game dir>\<the title's exe> <game dir>\XboxBvhBuilder.dll <game dir>\amd_fidelityfx_upscaler_xbox.dll
```

Run the title:

```powershell
cargo build --release -p victory
target\release\victory.exe run <game dir>\<the title's exe> --execute
```

Or, for normal play with the log in `%TEMP%\victory-play.log` (clears debugging
switches such as `VICTORY_KEYS` / `VICTORY_CAPTURE` first):

```powershell
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File scripts\play.ps1 -Game "<game dir>\<the title's exe>"
```

The window caption shows the framerate. Saves, settings and `crashes.log`
are kept under the data folder (default `%LOCALAPPDATA%\victory\data`), derived
data under the cache folder.

Input: XInput pads. With none connected the keyboard is a pad while the
title's window has focus:

| Keys | Pad |
|------|-----|
| W A S D / I J K L | left / right stick |
| arrows | D-pad |
| Enter, Backspace, X, Y | A, B, X, Y |
| Q / E, Z / C | shoulders, triggers |
| F / H | stick clicks |
| Esc, Tab | Menu, View |

## Commands

```
victory inspect <exe|dll> [--imports] [--exports]
victory census [paths...] [--missing] [--strict]
victory lib-exports <import.lib> [--list]
victory shader-info [paths...] [--verbose] [--disasm]
victory translate [paths...] [--validate] [--pipelines] [--list] [--jobs N]
victory translate [paths...] --cache [--pipelines [--used-pipelines]] [--jobs N]
victory run <exe> [--execute] [--unimplemented trap|zero] [--strict]
            [--console series-x|series-s] [-- <title arguments>]
victory config
```

Logging: `VICTORY_LOG=victory_loader=trace,info` (a release build logs
info and above).

## Debugging

A release build has no debugging aids. Build them in with

```powershell
cargo build --profile diag -p victory --features diagnostics   # target\diag\victory.exe
```

| Variable | Effect |
|----------|--------|
| `VICTORY_LOG=<target>=debug,...` | debug and trace output |
| `VICTORY_STACKS=20,60` | every thread's stack at those seconds |
| `VICTORY_WATCH=<rva>,...` | logs a title u32 when it changes |
| `VICTORY_KEYS=30:Enter,35:X` | presses keys at those seconds, focus or not |
| `VICTORY_PRESS_FILE=<file>` | (release builds too) presses what is appended to the file: pad buttons, sticks `LSUP`..`RSRIGHT`, `LT` `RT` `LS` `RS`; `NAME:MS` holds, `+NAME`/`-NAME` keep a key held and let it go |
| `VICTORY_CRASH_SAVE=<rva>:<len>,...` | saves title memory to the cache folder on a crash |
| `VICTORY_CAPTURE=<dir>` | saves presented frames (powers of two, or `VICTORY_CAPTURE_FRAMES=<list>`) |
| `VICTORY_CAPTURE_SYNC=1` | finishes each presented frame's PNG before presenting it (normally PNG encoding runs on a worker) |
| `VICTORY_DUMP_TARGETS=1` | with the two above, saves every render target of those frames |
| `VICTORY_D3D12_DEBUG=1` | host debug layer |
| `VICTORY_D3D12_GBV=1` | with the above, GPU-based validation (bad descriptor use reported, not faulted; slow) |
| `VICTORY_D3D12_DRED=1` | on a GPU failure, the allocation hit and the commands in flight |
| `VICTORY_PIPELINE_WAIT_MS=24` | reduce the per-frame wait for background pipeline creation from the default of 500 ms. A draw can be omitted until its pipeline is ready |
| `VICTORY_SKIP_PIPELINES=<hash>,...` | omit draws and dispatches, including indirect ones, whose pipeline contains a listed 16-digit shader hash |
| `VICTORY_GPU_TIMING=1` | each list of a submission run and timed on its own (no relay, so the frame rate is not the game's); every command's card time summed by pipeline, our own lists (card sync, preludes, card compare) by their waits; slow lists logged with their slowest commands. No `VICTORY_D3D12_DRED` needed (it costs card time of its own) |
| `VICTORY_GPU_TIMING_RANK=10` | with the above, every that many seconds (0: never) all the sums as `gpu timing rank` lines; `powershell -File scripts/gpu_rank.ps1 <run folder>` ranks a window of them (default the last 120 s) |
| `VICTORY_D3D12_WARP=1` | host device on the software adapter (slow; cannot reset the driver) |
| `VICTORY_SHADER_LOOP_LIMIT=<n>` | with diagnostics, override the default loop caps (128 pixel, 256 vertex/mesh/compute) with `n` iterations per lane; a lower cap can avoid GPU timeouts but also cuts off genuine long loops; own shader cache entries |
| `VICTORY_AUDIO_CAPTURE=<file.wav>` | records what the title plays (our process only) |
| `VICTORY_XMA_DUMP=<dir>` | saves the first 32 XMA voices' context and input |

Every build appends crashes and host GPU failures to `crashes.log` in the
title's data folder.

### Switches from the victory-fork VIwork port (#32)

On by default (each can be turned off): `VICTORY_VIEW_GUARD=0` (a write
through one view of a page is seen at its others), `VICTORY_HEAP_RECHECK=0`
(textures picked by index looked at again after their upload),
`VICTORY_TEXTURE_SWEEP=0` (lost views put back), `VICTORY_MOUSE=0` (mouse
look: a click in the window captures the mouse, Alt or focus loss lets it
go; `VICTORY_MOUSE_FULL`, `_SMOOTH_MS`, `_DEADZONE`, `_INVERT=1`).

Off by default, for A/B runs: `VICTORY_FILL_ON_CARD=1`,
`VICTORY_SPLIT_UNWATCHED=1`, `VICTORY_CARD_BATCH=1`, `VICTORY_COPY_COMPARE=1`,
`VICTORY_FULL_REFRESH=1|check`, `VICTORY_EARLY_TEXTURES_MS=12`,
`VICTORY_BAKE_TEXTURES_MS=1500`, `VICTORY_TEXTURE_ROOM_PERCENT=<50..97>`,
`VICTORY_PLACED_FILLER=0`, `VICTORY_RT_OFF=1` (the ray-tracing passes not
run at all, for comparison with the default clean-miss tracing off). Each
is described where it is read.


## Fork notes: measured progress on one RTX 3060 12 GB (2026-11-24)

Work on this fork, measured with `VICTORY_GPU_TIMING=1` and a frame grabber,
same scene each time (the title's opening world, a city street):

| change | before | after |
| --- | --- | --- |
| texture refresh without waiting on the remap lock (`VICTORY_TEXTURE_NO_WAIT=1`) | 312–545 ms a frame in texture work | 12–23 ms |
| card-buffer copies kept on the card (`VICTORY_CARD_BUFFERS_MIN_MB=8`, `VICTORY_CARD_BUFFERS_MB=1536`) | heap pass 262 ms, 1 906 draws | heap pass 15–24 ms, 4 833 draws |
| both together | 1.3–1.8 frames a second | 2.5–4 frames a second |

The menu runs at a locked 30. Saves persist across restarts. Cutscenes render
with light and subtitles.

Wave64 → wave32: a console wave64 program runs on a 32-lane host as two host
waves when its cross-lane code stays inside each half and its shared memory
is ordered. This fork adds an analysis that also halves programs whose every
shared-memory access names only the lane's own words (the address derives
from the thread id), which needs no ordering at all: of 1 163 dumped compute
programs, 37 more now run in waves of 32 than did before. The probe lives in
`crates/victory-shader/examples` (`wave_why`, `lds_dump`, `validate_dump`).

